  "status": "Approved",
  "description": "A sculpture of a large leaf, resting on the grass by artist Saad Qureshi for The Line. It is the artist's response to the unique landscape and history of Three Mills Island in Newham. The site is shaped by its position in the middle of narrow waterways, characterised by trees, winds and shifting tides along the River Lea. Fabricated in Corten steel, this sculptural leaf alludes to its function as a measure of time. The changing of seasons heralded by the turning of leaves before they fall to the ground is a potent poetic image shared globally. The symbolism of leaves is also notable in Islamic and Christian theology, to communicate with God, to convey romance or to share knowledge. The location was selected by the artist as it offers a space for reflection close to nearby trees and the water’s edge. \n\nAs part of the design process for his commission, Qureshi took part in a microresidency at V&A East Storehouse in February 2026. He was given access to the V&A's extraordinary collection over a series of four days, which culminated with him sharing the artefacts and stories that he encountered as part of V&A East's Look What I Found series. Over 650 visitors interacted with this free event over the course of an afternoon.\n\nThe Line intends to launch the installation during London Sculpture Week in September 2026. This initiative celebrates the capital’s vibrant creative spirit by transforming public spaces with striking outdoor sculptures. It unites four major cultural initiatives – Frieze Sculpture, the Fourth Plinth and GLA, Sculpture in the City, and The Line – to invigorate the city’s public spaces and highlight the transformative power of public art in the city. \n\nApprox dims: 100 x 550 x 200cm (HxLxD)\nIncludes accompanying signage as detailed in the Design and Access Statement.\n\nIt will be sited on a currently unoccupied grassy patch.",
